Ladybird completes every extension API call through settle_api_call(),
which resolves a promise or invokes a callback. A failed call
invokes its callback with no arguments, and runtime.lastError is set
only while that callback runs; the enclosing value comes back afterward,
even when callbacks nest. The value lives on the messaging context,
and an exception the callback throws goes to the console through
HTML::report_exception().

Chromium does the same in APIRequestHandler::AsyncResultHandler::
ResolveRequest(), which tells callbacks from promises, sets
APILastError around the callback, and clears it afterward.
Gecko scopes the error with BaseContext.withLastError(), clearing it
in a finally clause, and builds promises with BaseContext.wrapPromise(),
both in ExtensionCommon.sys.mjs. And WebKit scopes m_lastError around
its callback handler, through WebExtensionAPIRuntimeBase::reportError().
So all three match Ladybird’s callback and rejection behavior; restoring
the enclosing value, rather than clearing it, also keeps an outer
callback’s error when callbacks nest.
